<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Michael Psellos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1018–1078</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Michael Psellus, Psellos, Constantine Psellos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Michael Psellos was a Byzantine monk, writer, philosopher, historian and imperial courtier who advised several 11th-century emperors. Born Constantine, probably in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in 1017 or 1018, he rose from the imperial chancellery to advise Constantine IX Monomachos and to be the leading professor at the University of Constantinople, and he played a decisive part in the changes of ruler of 1057, 1059 and 1071. His Chronographia, biographies of 14 emperors and empresses from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> to Michael VII Doukas, is a valuable source on the 11th-century empire, and his revival of classical studies later influenced the Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. Most scholars believe he died soon after 1078.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/michael-psellos.jpg" alt="Michael Psellos" width="330" height="263" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown/Άγνωστος · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Michael_Psellos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Psellos's family came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedia">Nicomedia</a> and, by his own testimony, counted members of the consular and patrician elite among its ancestors. His baptismal name was Constantine; Michael was the monastic name he took on entering a monastery later. Psellos, meaning the stammerer, was probably a personal by-name referring to a speech defect. At about ten he was sent to work outside the capital as secretary to a provincial judge, to help raise a dowry for his sister. When she died he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> to study under John Mauropus, where he met the later patriarchs Constantine Leichoudes and John Xiphilinos and the later emperor Constantine X Doukas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After a further spell as a provincial judge, he returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> before 1042 as a junior secretary in the imperial chancellery and rose quickly. He became an influential adviser to Constantine IX Monomachos, who reigned from 1042 to 1055, and the leading professor at the University of Constantinople, with the title Chief of the Philosophers. Under political pressure he entered the Olympus monastery in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bithynia">Bithynia</a> in 1054, and Empress Theodora recalled him to court after Monomachos died.</p><p>He remained an adviser to successive emperors and played a decisive part in the transfers of power from Michael VI to Isaac I Komnenos in 1057, from Isaac to Constantine X Doukas in 1059 and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iv-diogenes">Romanos IV Diogenes</a> to Michael VII Doukas in 1071. He had taught Michael, but after the mid-1070s nothing is recorded of his role at court, and some scholars think he withdrew again to a monastery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Chronographia, his best-known work, is a series of biographies of fourteen emperors and empresses. It stresses character over political and military detail, gives most weight to the periods when Psellos was himself in politics and has the character of a political memoir. The second part is more apologetic and largely praises the Doukas emperors. He also wrote a shorter world chronicle, the Historia syntomos, treatises on philosophy, theology, astronomy, medicine, music and law, a treatise on demons, didactic poems, funeral orations, speeches and several hundred letters.</p><p>He prided himself on reviving serious study of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> and ancient philosophy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, and he read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a> and Plato as precursors of Christian doctrine. His fondness for pagan and Neoplatonic thinkers led some contemporaries to doubt his orthodoxy: in 1054 John Xiphilinos accused him of forsaking Christ to follow Plato, and he made a public profession of faith. Modern commentators have called him vain and opportunistic, while others find in his work an ironic undercurrent critical of the emperors he describes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Michael Psellos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Learning was the usual way in: <b>Michael Psellos</b> began as a secretary and became adviser to several emperors.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1057" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Michael Psellos"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4934 4064h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4934 4064h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=1057">What was here in 1057? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
